1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:11,180
Our teacher, Miss Chevalier, was a small woman, with a moon face, plump fingers and curls that sprang straight up from her head, hence the funny name “Poodle”.
我们的老师谢瓦利埃小姐是个身材娇小的女人，圆圆的脸，手指胖乎乎的，一头卷发直直地竖在头上，因此有了“卷毛狗”这个滑稽的外号。

2
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:13,560
She taught in our reading club.
她在我们的读书俱乐部教课。

3
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:18,730
Sometimes she would look in and ask what we were reading — not to check but to know.
有时她会进来看看，问我们在读什么——不是为了检查，而是想了解。

4
00:00:19,530 --> 00:00:23,800
That’s what happened the day my club was reading aloud a poem by Henry Longfellow.
我们俱乐部朗读亨利·朗费罗的一首诗那天，就发生了这样的事。

5
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:33,480
I guess I was better than the others, for Miss Chevalier asked a while later, “Addie, I was wondering if you would recite the whole poem to the Saturday evening club.”
我想我读得比其他人好，因为过了一会儿，谢瓦利埃小姐问道：“艾迪，我在想，你愿不愿意在周六晚间俱乐部活动上背诵整首诗。”

6
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:41,490
She said a famous professor was going to give a lecture about the poet, and a presentation of that poem would be a fresh way to start.
她说，一位著名教授要来做一场关于这位诗人的讲座，而以朗诵那首诗来开场会很新颖。

7
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:43,790
She asked me to memorize it.
她让我把这首诗背下来。

8
00:00:44,590 --> 00:00:48,020
“But that shouldn’t be a problem for a child of your ability,” she added.
“不过对于像你这样有能力的孩子来说，这应该不成问题，”她补充道。

9
00:00:48,820 --> 00:00:52,810
I’m telling you, my happy feet barely touched the ground all the way home.
说真的，回家的一路上，我高兴得脚都快不沾地了。

10
00:00:53,610 --> 00:01:00,530
To me, that was the biggest thing ever and I soon learned the whole poem by heart, well prepared for our first rehearsal.
对我来说，那是我经历过的最了不起的事。我很快就把整首诗背得滚瓜烂熟，为第一次排练做好了充分准备。

11
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:04,680
I was desperately nervous when I went to her office the next day.
第二天去她办公室时，我紧张得要命。

12
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:10,440
But the good thing was she always had the smile that would make you feel like you just did something right.
不过好在她总是带着那种微笑，让你觉得自己好像刚做对了什么事。

13
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:16,320
Halfway through the poem, Miss Chevalier stopped me and asked if I knew what “impetuous” meant.
诗背到一半时，谢瓦利埃小姐打断了我，问我知不知道“impetuous”是什么意思。

14
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:24,660
Despite her encouraging smile, I wanted to sink through the floor because I was unsure about its pronunciation as well as its meaning.
尽管她带着鼓励的微笑，我还是恨不得找个地缝钻进去，因为这个词的读音和意思我都没有把握。

15
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:32,940
Miss Chevalier pretended not to have noticed my red face and handed me a dictionary, asking me to read the word’s definition aloud.
谢瓦利埃小姐假装没注意到我涨红的脸，递给我一本词典，让我把这个词的释义大声读出来。

16
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:40,604
“Impetuous has two definitions: rushing with great force or violence; acting suddenly, with little thought.”
“impetuous有两个释义：一是以巨大的力量或猛烈之势向前冲；二是行事突然，几乎不加思考。”

17
00:01:41,404 --> 00:01:43,454
She asked me which one fitted the poem.
她问我哪一个释义符合诗中的意思。

18
00:01:44,254 --> 00:01:50,354
I read both definitions over again, trying to figure it out, but Miss Chevalier must have read my mind.
我把两个释义又读了一遍，想弄明白，但谢瓦利埃小姐一定是看出了我的心思。

19
00:01:51,154 --> 00:01:52,854
“There is no wrong answer,” she said.
“怎么回答都不算错，”她说。

20
00:01:53,654 --> 00:01:55,864
“I want to know your opinion, Addie.”
“我想知道你的看法，艾迪。”

21
00:01:56,664 --> 00:01:59,344
Hesitantly, I said, “Maybe… both.”
我犹豫地说：“也许……两个都是。”

22
00:02:00,144 --> 00:02:01,094
She liked that.
她喜欢这个回答。

23
00:02:01,894 --> 00:02:06,674
“One has to be impetuous both ways or one wouldn’t dare to face up to any challenge.
“一个人得在两种意义上都‘冲动’才行，否则就不敢直面任何挑战。

24
00:02:07,474 --> 00:02:09,184
Would you call yourself impetuous?”
你会说自己是个冲动的人吗？”

25
00:02:09,984 --> 00:02:12,084
I knew she was asking for an opinion.
我知道她是在征求我的看法。

26
00:02:12,884 --> 00:02:17,224
“My mother thinks it is improper for girls to be impetuous, anyway.”
“反正我妈妈认为女孩子冲动是不得体的。”

27
00:02:18,024 --> 00:02:20,334
She said my mom was somewhat right about that.
她说我妈妈在这一点上有几分道理。

28
00:02:21,134 --> 00:02:24,594
“But girls should also be wise enough to take up challenges when needed.
“但女孩子也应该足够明智，在需要时接受挑战。

29
00:02:25,394 --> 00:02:26,954
I believe you are such a girl.”
我相信你就是这样的女孩。”

30
00:02:27,754 --> 00:02:31,314
After that, I would never call Miss Chevalier “Poodle” again.
从那以后，我再也不叫谢瓦利埃小姐“卷毛狗”了。
